<!-- WHY AEROSOL CAN SAFETY MATTERS FOR STARTUPS & MANUFACTURERS -->
<section class="section" id="why-aerosol-can-safety-matters-for-startups-manufacturers">
  <h2>Why Aerosol Can Safety Matters for Startups & Manufacturers</h2>
  <p>Aerosol cans operate as sealed pressure vessels, meaning their safety depends on predictable mechanical
                behavior under defined loads. Startups entering the aerosol ecosystem often underestimate the
                engineering behind can selection, pressure design, and testing. In practice, safety failures arise not
                from rare circumstances but from basic errors such as incorrect propellant ratios, improper fill levels,
                or the use of non-certified components.</p>
            <p>A reliable safety strategy reduces the risk of can rupture, valve failure, product recalls, and
                regulatory violations. In regions like India, where the aerosol market is expanding rapidly, adherence
                to global standards is essential for exports and brand credibility.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Predictable pressure behavior across temperature ranges</li>
                <li>Mechanical strength of tinplate or aluminium cans</li>
                <li>Regulatory acceptance for transport and storage</li>
                <li>Quality control across every filling batch</li>
            </ul>
</section>

<!-- UNDERSTANDING AEROSOL CAN PRESSURE BASICS -->
<section class="section" id="understanding-aerosol-can-pressure-basics">
  <h2>Understanding Aerosol Can Pressure Basics</h2>
        <p>Pressure behaviour differs fundamentally between propellant types, a distinction covered in full in our guide to <a href="/compressed-vs-liquefied-gas-aerosols-pressure-profiles" style="color: var(--primary); font-weight: 600;">compressed versus liquefied gas aerosols</a> and <a href="/how-aerosol-cans-work" style="color: var(--primary); font-weight: 600;">how aerosol cans work</a>.</p>
  <p>Aerosol pressure arises primarily from the vapor pressure of the propellant inside the container.
                Propellants such as LPG (propane–butane mixtures), DME, CO₂, and HFC-152a each exert specific vapor
                pressures at given temperatures. Since aerosols are stored and transported at varying temperatures,
                pressure calculations must consider worst-case conditions such as 50–55°C.</p>
            <p>Internal pressure increases sharply with temperature due to the thermodynamic expansion of gases and
                increased vaporization of liquefied propellants. Standard tinplate cans are engineered with a defined
                working pressure, test pressure, and burst pressure to ensure safe operation.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Tinplate 2-piece and 3-piece cans: working pressure ≈ 8–10 bar at 55°C</li>
                <li>Aluminium monobloc cans: working pressure ≈ 12–15 bar at 55°C</li>
                <li>Propellants like propane–butane mixtures: vapor pressure ≈ 6–8 bar at 20°C, rising at higher
                    temperatures</li>
            </ul>

            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Parameter</th>
                        <th>Typical Range / Note</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Working pressure (tinplate cans)</td>
                        <td>≈ 8–10 bar at 55°C</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Working pressure (aluminium cans)</td>
                        <td>≈ 12–15 bar at 55°C</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Propellant vapor pressure (propane–butane)</td>
                        <td>≈ 6–8 bar at 20°C (increases with temp)</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
</section>

<!-- HOW TO PERFORM AEROSOL PRESSURE CALCULATIONS -->
<section class="section" id="how-to-perform-aerosol-pressure-calculations">
  <h2>How to Perform Aerosol Pressure Calculations</h2>
  <p>Accurate pressure calculations use vapor pressure data from established physical property tables for each
                propellant. These values are typically presented as temperature–pressure curves. The internal pressure
                is determined by the vapor pressure of the propellant in equilibrium with its liquid phase, assuming the
                fill ratio is within specification.</p>
            <p>The fill ratio—the percentage of liquid in the can—affects the gas space available to absorb thermal
                expansion. Higher liquid fill ratios leave less headspace and can cause pressure to rise more sharply at
                elevated temperatures. Therefore, quantitative pressure modelling is essential during formulation.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Use validated vapor pressure curves or Antoine equation data for each propellant</li>
                <li>Consider the mixture rule for blended LPG systems</li>
                <li>Maintain recommended liquid fill ratios (typically ≤ 80%)</li>
                <li>Ensure working pressure is well below the can’s certified test and burst pressures</li>
            </ul>

            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Calculation Consideration</th>
                        <th>Recommendation</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Vapor pressure data source</td>
                        <td>Validated tables or Antoine equation</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Fill ratio</td>
                        <td>Typically ≤ 80% liquid</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Mixtures (LPG blends)</td>
                        <td>Apply mixture rules for vapor pressure</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
</section>

<!-- BURST TESTING EXPLAINED (THE ULTIMATE CAN SAFETY TEST) -->
<section class="section" id="burst-testing-explained-the-ultimate-can-safety-test">
  <h2>Burst Testing Explained (The Ultimate Can Safety Test)</h2>
        <p>Burst testing verifies the container independently of the seal; the crimp and gasket side of the safety envelope is covered separately in <a href="/aerosol-valve-gasket-explained" style="color: var(--primary); font-weight: 600;">aerosol valve gasket materials</a> and <a href="/why-do-aerosol-cans-leak" style="color: var(--primary); font-weight: 600;">why aerosol cans leak</a>.</p>
  <p>Burst testing is the definitive mechanical test that ensures an aerosol can can withstand extreme
                internal pressures beyond normal operating conditions. The test simulates a worst-case scenario by
                gradually increasing pressure—usually with water in hydrostatic equipment—until the can mechanically
                fails. This provides a measurable burst pressure, which must exceed regulatory minimums.</p>
            <p>Hydrostatic burst testing is the preferred method because water is incompressible and reduces the risk of
                explosive failure during testing. Pneumatic tests may be used for specific components but require strict
                safety controls due to stored energy in compressed air.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Tinplate cans generally require burst values around 18–22 bar</li>
                <li>Aluminium monobloc cans often exceed 18–20 bar</li>
                <li>Test pressure (hydrostatic) is usually 1.5 times the working pressure</li>
                <li>Consistent failure mode indicates stable manufacturing quality</li>
            </ul>

            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Test</th>
                        <th>Typical Requirement / Note</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Hydrostatic burst</td>
                        <td>Tinplate ~18–22 bar; Aluminium >18–20 bar</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Test pressure multiplier</td>
                        <td>Usually 1.5× working pressure</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Pneumatic testing</td>
                        <td>Use with strict safety controls</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

