                        <td>USA aerosol pressure vessel classification</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>UN Model Regulations</td>
                        <td>International transport of dangerous goods</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>ADR</td>
                        <td>European road transport of dangerous goods</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>BIS</td>
                        <td>Indian standards for metal containers</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
</section>

<!-- DOT GUIDELINES: WHAT INDIAN & GLOBAL BRANDS MUST KNOW -->
<section class="section" id="dot-guidelines-what-indian-global-brands-must-know">
  <h2>DOT Guidelines: What Indian & Global Brands Must Know</h2>
        <p>Full classification and packaging requirements for shipping aerosols as UN 1950 dangerous goods are set out in the <a href="https://unece.org/transport/dangerous-goods" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: var(--primary); font-weight: 600;">UN Model Regulations</a>, and covered in depth in our guide to <a href="/dot-un-aerosol-transport-regulations-hazmat" style="color: var(--primary); font-weight: 600;">DOT and UN aerosol transport rules</a>.</p>
  <p>DOT classifications directly relate to the minimum strength and test performance required from aerosol
                cans. DOT-2P applies to certain lightweight tinplate cans, while DOT-2Q applies to higher-strength,
                pressurized containers. These markings indicate that the can meets defined test and burst pressure
                requirements under federal regulations.</p>
            <p>DOT guidelines also mandate hazard communication, labeling, leak testing, hot-water bath verification,
                and documentation. Hot-water bath testing at 50–55°C is widely used in manufacturing to ensure no leaks
                or deformation occur at elevated pressure.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>2P and 2Q designations specify certified minimum pressure performance</li>
                <li>Every filled can must pass leak detection and hot-water bath testing</li>
                <li>Pressure standards are aligned with temperature exposure risk</li>
                <li>Transport regulations require proper labels, symbols, and documentation</li>
            </ul>

            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>DOT Parameter</th>
                        <th>Requirement / Note</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>DOT designation</td>
                        <td>2P or 2Q (certified minimum performance)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Hot-water bath</td>
                        <td>50–55°C to check leaks/deformation</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Labeling</td>
                        <td>Hazard communication and transport docs required</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
</section>

<!-- SAFETY BEST PRACTICES FOR AEROSOL STARTUPS -->
<section class="section" id="safety-best-practices-for-aerosol-startups">
  <h2>Safety Best Practices for Aerosol Startups</h2>
        <p>These practices are formalised across the manufacturing stages described in <a href="/aerosol-manufacturing-101-guide" style="color: var(--primary); font-weight: 600;">Aerosol Manufacturing 101</a> and the scale-up sequence in <a href="/from-lab-to-commercial-launch" style="color: var(--primary); font-weight: 600;">from lab to commercial launch</a>.</p>
  <p>New aerosol manufacturers frequently face safety issues due to improper propellant selection, inaccurate
                filling, or insufficient quality control. A structured approach ensures safe operations and maintains
                compliance with domestic and international regulations.</p>
            <p>Proper can selection must match the internal pressure profile of the formulation. The propellant
                concentration must be validated through pressure testing, and filling lines must incorporate reliable
                crimping, gassing, and leak-testing systems.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Select cans (tinplate or aluminium) based on validated pressure data</li>
                <li>Use propellants appropriate for the formulation’s performance and safety</li>
                <li>Ensure accurate crimping and valve integrity checking on every batch</li>
                <li>Maintain QC logs including weight checks, pressure tests, and leak tests</li>
            </ul>

            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Best Practice</th>
                        <th>Action</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Can selection</td>
                        <td>Match can to validated pressure profile</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Propellant validation</td>
                        <td>Pressure testing for concentration</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Crimping & valves</td>
                        <td>Integrity checks every batch</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>QC documentation</td>
                        <td>Weight, pressure, leak test logs</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
</section>

<!-- COMMON MISTAKES MADE BY NEW MANUFACTURERS (AND HOW TO AVOID THEM) -->
<section class="section" id="common-mistakes-made-by-new-manufacturers-and-how-to-avoid-them">
  <h2>Common Mistakes Made by New Manufacturers (And How to Avoid Them)</h2>
  <p>Many aerosol safety issues stem from preventable errors during formulation and filling. Overfilling
                liquid or using excessive LPG ratios dramatically increases internal pressure, especially under elevated
                temperatures. Similarly, non-certified cans or valves significantly reduce safety margins.</p>
            <p>Hot-water bath testing is sometimes incorrectly performed or bypassed, leading to undetected leaks. These
                oversights can result in product failures during transport or consumer use.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Overfilling leading to inadequate headspace</li>
                <li>Using non-compliant cans, valves, or components</li>
                <li>Incorrect crimping force causing leaks under pressure</li>
                <li>Skipping routine QC testing such as pressure and hot bath checks</li>
            </ul>

            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Common Mistake</th>
                        <th>How to Avoid</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Overfilling</td>
                        <td>Adhere to fill ratio recommendations</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Non-compliant components</td>
                        <td>Use certified cans/valves</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Incorrect crimping</td>
                        <td>Calibrate crimping tools and check seals</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Skipping QC</td>
                        <td>Enforce batch testing protocols</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
</section>

