      <!-- FAQ -->
      <section class="section" id="faq" style="margin-top: 50px;">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-md); margin-bottom: 10px; background: #ffffff;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 600; padding: 14px 18px; cursor: pointer;">What is the normal working pressure of an aerosol can?</summary>
          <div style="padding: 0 18px 16px; color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.65;">Typical service pressure at 55 degrees Celsius is around 8 to 10 bar for tinplate cans and 12 to 15 bar for aluminium cans, though the exact figure depends on the propellant and fill ratio used. This is the pressure the container must withstand throughout its labelled temperature range, not just at room temperature. Design margin above this working pressure is what the burst test verifies.</div>
        </details>

        <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-md); margin-bottom: 10px; background: #ffffff;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 600; padding: 14px 18px; cursor: pointer;">Why is burst testing required for aerosol cans?</summary>
          <div style="padding: 0 18px 16px; color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.65;">Burst testing confirms the container fails only well above its normal operating pressure, giving a documented safety margin rather than an assumption. Regulators require it because a can that ruptures in transport or storage is a direct hazard to people and property. It is destructive testing performed on production samples, not on every unit shipped.</div>
        </details>

        <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-md); margin-bottom: 10px; background: #ffffff;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 600; padding: 14px 18px; cursor: pointer;">What temperature is used for aerosol hot water bath testing?</summary>
          <div style="padding: 0 18px 16px; color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.65;">The standard hot water bath test brings the can's contents to 50 to 55 degrees Celsius, simulating the elevated temperatures a can can realistically reach during transport or warehousing. Every filled can is required to pass through this bath without leaking or bursting before it leaves the production line. It is a 100 percent inspection, unlike burst testing which is done on samples.</div>
        </details>

        <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-md); margin-bottom: 10px; background: #ffffff;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 600; padding: 14px 18px; cursor: pointer;">How does propellant choice affect aerosol can safety?</summary>
          <div style="padding: 0 18px 16px; color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.65;">Each propellant follows its own vapour pressure curve, so the same can design will run at a different internal pressure depending on which propellant is used and at what fill ratio. Choosing a propellant without checking that pressure envelope against the container's rated burst pressure removes the safety margin the design assumed. This is why propellant and container are always qualified together, not independently.</div>
        </details>

        <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-md); margin-bottom: 10px; background: #ffffff;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 600; padding: 14px 18px; cursor: pointer;">Can non-certified aerosol cans be legally sold?</summary>
          <div style="padding: 0 18px 16px; color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.65;">No, in the markets that matter commercially. Aerosols are regulated as dangerous goods for transport, and both DOT in the United States and the UN Model Regulations internationally require the container to meet defined pressure and testing standards before it can be shipped. Using an uncertified can also removes the manufacturer's basis for the safety margin the whole product design relies on.</div>
        </details>

        <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-md); margin-bottom: 10px; background: #ffffff;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 600; padding: 14px 18px; cursor: pointer;">What happens if an aerosol can is overfilled?</summary>
          <div style="padding: 0 18px 16px; color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.65;">Overfilling reduces the headspace available for the propellant vapour to expand into as temperature rises, which pushes internal pressure toward the can's design limit faster than intended. Because liquids are effectively incompressible, there is very little margin once headspace is lost. Correct fill weight and volume are therefore treated as safety controls, checked continuously on the filling line rather than only at design stage.</div>
        </details>
      </section>

